INV-lizor limb (a). Name-based mapping has a floor: a test named for the
BEHAVIOUR it pins, or covering several sources, or whose subject is a
pipeline YAML rather than a source at all, is unreachable by any rule of
that shape. The map's docstring promised a declarative marker and
KNOWN_UNREACHABLE asked for it by name in entry after entry -- "these want
a declarative covers: marker, not a heuristic".

A root test now names its own subjects in its header:

    # covers: .woodpecker/*.yml, scripts/lib/forgejo-api.sh

Every changed path matching one of those globs selects it, beside the name
rules rather than instead of them. Twenty-two tests declare; the exempt
list drops 25 -> 3. Every marker was derived by READING the test -- the
paths it opens, the module it loads by file path -- because a name-based
rule could not reach these and a wrong declaration is worse than none.

WHAT IS LEFT IS WHAT A DECLARATION CANNOT HONESTLY EXPRESS: a test whose
subject is another TEST. No source change breaks those.

A DECLARATION MAY ADD A TEST AND MAY NEVER WITHDRAW OVER-SELECTION, and
that arrived as a failure rather than a design. smart-test unions the
WHOLE root suite for a top-level source the map cannot reach -- deliberate
over-selection for .githooks/** and scripts/lib/**, whose basenames are
too generic to map. The first marker naming .githooks/* made those paths
"mapped" and would have traded 108 tests for one. The existing decision
test caught it. The fallback asks the name rules (--names-only); the union
asks both.

CI CONFIG IS NO LONGER DARK. .woodpecker/ and .github/ join the executable
surface. Fifteen root tests read those files, and "editing a workflow does
NOT select this test per-PR" appears verbatim on four KNOWN_UNREACHABLE
entries without closing any of them.

The markers ratchet two-sidedly like the list they replace: deleting one
makes its test unreachable and fails, and a glob matching nothing in the
tree has rotted and fails. Both arms verified by mutation. The
unreachability ratchet now walks every tracked file rather than only the
paths the name rules claim, because a declaration can name anything.

